@startuml uart-send-bounded
' Why every wait on a transmitter flag is counted. Grounded in Src/uart.c
' (wait_for_flag, uart_send_char, uart_send_string, uart_send_bytes) and in
' the watchdog period from Inc/watchdog.h.

title Sending a line, with no way to wait forever

skinparam shadowing false

start

:a line to send;

repeat
  :next character;

  while (TXE set?) is (no)
    :one more turn;
    if (turns used up?) then (yes)
      :give up on this byte;
      note right
        The transmitter is not working.
        The rest of the line is dropped
        rather than spending the whole
        limit again on every character.
      end note
      :return, loop carries on;
      stop
    else (no)
    endif
  endwhile (yes)

  :write the byte to TDR;
repeat while (more characters?) is (yes)

:done;

stop

legend bottom
  Counted in TURNS, not milliseconds, and deliberately so. This runs on
  paths where the millisecond clock is not started yet, and a stopped
  clock is one of the faults the bound exists to survive. A guard that
  leans on the thing it guards against is not a guard.

  100000 turns is roughly 0.17 s at 4 MHz, about two thousand byte times
  at 115200. Far past any wait a working transmitter needs, far short of
  the 32.8 s watchdog. That gap is the point: the main loop gets back to
  feeding the dog instead of dying inside a send.
endlegend

@enduml
